\section{The [[assignment]] command (convenience alias)}
\label{sec:assignment-alias}

We provide an [[assignment]] (singular) command that serves as a direct alias
for [[assignments view]].
This serves two important purposes:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Backwards compatibility}: Before the refactoring to nested
  subcommands, the CLI had a standalone [[assignment]] command. By providing
  this alias, we ensure that existing scripts using [[canvaslms assignment]]
  continue to work without modification, preventing breaking changes for users
  who haven't migrated to the new [[assignments view]] syntax.
\item \textbf{Convenience and natural language}: Both singular and plural forms
  feel natural when working with assignments. The singular form is particularly
  intuitive when viewing a single assignment's details.
\end{itemize}

The command reuses all the same options and functionality as [[assignments view]],
simply providing a shorter, more natural command name when working with
individual assignments.

\subsection{An alias must declare every option its command function reads}

Sharing the command function is what makes the alias faithful, but it is also
what makes an incomplete option list fatal.  Argparse creates an attribute in
the resulting [[Namespace]] only for the options that \emph{this} parser
declares; [[assignments_view_command]], however, was written against the
[[view]] parser and reads [[args.html]] unconditionally to choose between HTML
and Markdown output.  An alias that registers the function without the option
therefore does not simply offer fewer options---it raises [[AttributeError]] on
every successful lookup.  Since [[main()]] only catches [[EmptyListError]] and
[[MultipleMatchesError]], the user sees a raw traceback, and precisely the old
scripts the alias exists to protect are the ones that break.

Calling [[add_html_option]] from \cref{sec:html-option} here, rather than
repeating its [[add_argument]] call, is what keeps the two parsers identical by
construction: a future option added to [[assignments view]] becomes one more
helper call to mirror, and the helper is the single place where its flag name,
default and help text are decided.
<<add assignment command to subp>>=
assignment_parser = subp.add_parser("assignment",
  help="View assignment details (alias for 'assignments view')",
  description="Views detailed information about assignments")
assignment_parser.set_defaults(func=assignments_view_command)
add_assignment_option(assignment_parser)
add_html_option(assignment_parser)
canvaslms.cli.links.add_link_options(assignment_parser)
@

\subsection{Verifying that the alias accepts the same options}

The regression is invisible to a parser-level smoke test that only checks that
[[canvaslms assignment]] exists, so we assert the property that actually
matters: parsing the same arguments through the alias and through
[[assignments view]] must yield namespaces with the same attributes, apart from
the [[assignments_subcommand]] field that only the nested form records.  That
formulation catches any future option that is added to one parser and forgotten
on the other, not just [[--html]].

<<test functions>>=
class TestAssignmentAlias:
    """Test that 'assignment' is a faithful alias for 'assignments view'."""

    def parse(self, argv):
        """Parse argv with the real assignments parsers."""
        parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
        subp = parser.add_subparsers()
        add_command(subp)
        return parser.parse_args(argv)

    def test_alias_defines_html_option(self):
        """The alias must provide the args.html the command function reads."""
        assert self.parse(["assignment", "-a", "Lab 1"]).html is False
        assert self.parse(["assignment", "--html", "-a", "Lab 1"]).html is True

    def test_alias_dispatches_to_view_command(self):
        """The alias must run the command function 'assignments view' runs."""
        assert self.parse(["assignment"]).func is assignments_view_command

    def test_alias_options_match_view_options(self):
        """Both spellings must produce namespaces with the same attributes."""
        alias_args = self.parse(["assignment", "-a", "Lab 1"])
        view_args = self.parse(["assignments", "view", "-a", "Lab 1"])

        assert vars(alias_args).keys() == (
            vars(view_args).keys() - {"assignments_subcommand"}
        )
@

Now, the [[assignments_list_command]] function implements the core logic for
listing assignments. It takes three arguments: [[config]], [[canvas]] and
[[args]] following the standard command pattern.

The function uses [[process_assignment_option]] to parse the filtering options 
and then outputs the results in CSV format for compatibility with UNIX tools.
