\section{Filter assignments and assignment groups of a course}

We want to filter out the assignment groups that match a regex.
<<functions>>=
def filter_assignment_groups(course, regex):
  """Returns all assignment groups of course whose name matches regex"""
  name = re.compile(regex)
  for group in course.get_assignment_groups():
    group = canvaslms.cli.utils.ensure_assignment_group_attrs(group)
    group.course = course
    if name.search(group.name or ''):
      yield group
@

\subsection{Naming an assignment group in a file}
\label{sec:assignment-group-spec}

The assignment group decides grading: [[results -A]] summarizes a group, so
an item in the wrong group silently drops out of one group's grade and into
another's.
A file must therefore be able to say which group its assignment or quiz
belongs to (issue~\#528), or a course round rebuilt from files puts every new
item in whatever group Canvas picks---the course's first---and the author has
to drag them into place in the web interface.

The file says it with an [[assignment_group]] key, for example
\begin{verbatim}
assignment_group: ^Participation INL1$
\end{verbatim}
Its value is a regex matched against the group names and Canvas IDs, the same
convention as [[regex]] and [[modules]] use, and for the same reason: Canvas
IDs change when a course is copied for the next round, names do not.
An export therefore writes the group's name, escaped and anchored, rather than
the [[assignment_group_id]] Canvas gives us.

Unlike [[modules]], where a regex may legitimately place an item in several
modules, an assignment is in exactly one group.
A spec that matches no group, or several, is therefore an error, reported
before anything is written---with one exception.
Group names need not be unique, and courses built by importing others often
have two groups called \enquote{Assignments}.
The exported spec [[^Assignments$]] then matches both, and refusing it would
make every unchanged round trip of such an item fail, the interactive editor
included, since it shows the spec too.
So the caller may say which group the item is in now: when the spec matches
several groups and the current one is among them, the item stays where it
is, which is all an unchanged file can mean.
A new item has no current group, so for it the ambiguity remains an error.

We raise [[ValueError]] for every failure, not the [[EmptyListError]] and
[[MultipleMatchesError]] that [[match_item_by_regex]] raises for items: the
callers already turn [[ValueError]] into a message for an unparseable date,
and an [[EmptyListError]] escaping into the file-edit code would be mistaken
for \enquote{no assignment matches} and trigger [[--create]].
A YAML file may give an ID as a bare number; we anchor it, because the regex
[[3]] would also match group 13.

A file names a second kind of course item the same way: the grading scheme
of a letter-graded assignment (\cref{sec:grading-standard-spec}).
The matching is therefore written for any items that have an ID and a name,
and [[key]] and [[kind]] only say, in the messages, which file key and which
kind of item the spec was meant for.
<<functions>>=
def resolve_named_item(items, spec, key, kind, current=None,
                       name_attr='name'):
  """Return the ID of the one item of `items` that `spec` names.

  `spec` is a regex matched against each item's `name_attr` and ID, or an
  integer ID. `key` and `kind` name the file key and the kind of item in
  error messages. `current` is the ID of the item in use now, if any; it
  is kept when `spec` matches several items including it.
  Raises ValueError when no item or several items match, or when the
  regex is invalid.
  """
  if isinstance(spec, int):
    spec = f"^{spec}$"
  try:
    pattern = re.compile(str(spec))
  except re.error as e:
    raise ValueError(f"{key}: invalid regex '{spec}': {e}")
  matches = [item for item in items
             if pattern.search(getattr(item, name_attr, None) or '')
             or pattern.search(str(item.id))]
  for item in matches:
    if current is not None and str(item.id) == str(current):
      return item.id
  if len(matches) == 1:
    return matches[0].id
  if not matches:
    raise ValueError(f"{key}: no {kind} matches '{spec}'")
  names = ", ".join(f"'{getattr(item, name_attr, None)}'" for item in matches)
  raise ValueError(f"{key}: '{spec}' matches {len(matches)} {kind}s "
                   f"({names}); make it more specific")

def resolve_assignment_group(course, spec, current=None):
  """Return the ID of the one assignment group of `course` matching `spec`.

  `spec` is a regex matched against group names and IDs, or an integer
  ID. `current` is the ID of the item's present group, if any; it is kept
  when `spec` matches several groups including it.
  Raises ValueError when no group or several groups match, or when the
  regex is invalid.
  """
  return resolve_named_item(course.get_assignment_groups(), spec,
                            'assignment_group', 'assignment group', current)
@

The export goes the other way: from the [[assignment_group_id]] of an
assignment or quiz to the spec a file carries.
An ID that names no group of the course (a group deleted since) gives no spec
rather than an error, because a view should still show the rest of the item.
We compare the IDs as strings, because the two APIs disagree on their type:
the Assignments and Classic Quizzes APIs give [[assignment_group_id]] as a
number, but the New Quizzes API gives it as a string ([["37658"]]), and a
numeric comparison silently found no group for any New Quiz.
The wrapper checks for a missing ID before it lists the groups, so an item
without one costs no request.
<<functions>>=
def named_item_spec(items, item_id, name_attr='name'):
  """Return an anchored regex naming the item of `items` with ID `item_id`.

  Returns None when no item has that ID.
  """
  for item in items:
    if str(item.id) == str(item_id):
      return '^' + re.escape(getattr(item, name_attr, None) or '') + '$'
  return None

def assignment_group_spec(course, group_id):
  """Return an anchored regex naming assignment group `group_id`.

  Returns None when `group_id` is None or names no group of `course`.
  """
  if group_id is None:
    return None
  return named_item_spec(course.get_assignment_groups(), group_id)
@

