We also want to filter out the assignments of a course that belong to a given
assignment group.
<<functions>>=
def filter_assignments_by_group(assignment_group, assignments):
  """Returns elements in assignments that are part of assignment_group"""
  for assignment in assignments:
    if assignment.assignment_group_id == assignment_group.id:
      assignment.assignment_group = assignment_group
      yield assignment
@

We need a variant that filters by a \emph{list} of assignment groups.
This is used for AND filtering: when [[-A]] matches multiple groups (via regex),
we want assignments in \emph{any} of those groups, as long as they also match
the other filters.

For example, if [[-A "Graded|Homework"]] matches both ``Graded'' and ``Homework''
groups, we return assignments from either group.
But if we also specify [[-a "Lab"]], we only return Lab assignments from those
groups.

The key difference from [[filter_assignments_by_group]] is that the singular
version receives one group object, whereas this one receives a whole sequence
of them---and that sequence must be traversed with care.
The name says \emph{list}, but [[filter_assignment_groups]] is a generator
function, so the caller above hands us a generator.
A generator can only be traversed once: whichever loop reads it first consumes
it, and every later loop sees an empty sequence.
An implementation that first collected the group identifiers for the membership
test and then rescanned the groups to find the matching object would therefore
work when handed a list and fail silently when handed a generator---the
membership test would be exhausted before the lookup ever ran, so
[[assignment.assignment_group]] would never be attached.

The failure is silent because the right assignments are still yielded; only the
attached group object goes missing.
The damage shows up one level up, in [[assignments_list_command]], whose
[[hasattr]] check then falls through to an extra [[get_assignment_group]] API
call per assignment---and prints the placeholder [[Group 12345]] instead of the
group's name whenever that call fails.

We avoid the trap by traversing the groups exactly once, into a dictionary
keyed by group identifier.
A single traversal is what makes the function correct for generators as well as
lists, and the dictionary answers both questions we have about an assignment's
group---is it among the filtered ones, and which object is it---in one constant
time lookup, instead of the linear rescan per assignment that a plain set of
identifiers would force.
<<functions>>=
def filter_assignments_by_group_list(assignment_groups, assignments):
  """Returns elements in assignments that belong to any of the assignment_groups"""
  groups_by_id = {group.id: group for group in assignment_groups}

  for assignment in assignments:
    group = groups_by_id.get(assignment.assignment_group_id)
    if group is not None:
      assignment.assignment_group = group
      yield assignment
@

\subsection{Verifying group attachment for generator input}

The regression we must guard against is exactly the one described above, so the
test feeds the function a generator rather than a list---a list would pass even
with the exhausted-generator bug---and asserts that the matching group object
was attached, not merely that the assignment survived the filter.

<<test functions>>=
class TestFilterAssignmentsByGroupList:
    """Test filtering assignments by a sequence of assignment groups."""

    def make_group(self, group_id, name):
        """Create a real Canvas assignment group instance."""
        return AssignmentGroup(MagicMock(), {"id": group_id, "name": name})

    def make_assignment(self, assignment_id, name, group_id):
        """Create a real Canvas assignment instance belonging to a group."""
        return Assignment(MagicMock(), {
            "id": assignment_id,
            "name": name,
            "assignment_group_id": group_id,
        })

    def test_attaches_group_when_given_a_generator(self):
        """A one-shot generator of groups must still attach the group object."""
        graded = self.make_group(1, "Graded")
        homework = self.make_group(2, "Homework")
        in_group = self.make_assignment(10, "Lab 1", 1)
        out_of_group = self.make_assignment(11, "Lab 2", 3)

        groups = (group for group in [graded, homework])
        result = list(filter_assignments_by_group_list(
            groups, [in_group, out_of_group]))

        assert result == [in_group]
        assert result[0].assignment_group is graded

    def test_attaches_group_when_given_a_list(self):
        """Lists behave like generators, so both call styles stay supported."""
        homework = self.make_group(2, "Homework")
        assignment = self.make_assignment(10, "Lab 1", 2)

        result = list(filter_assignments_by_group_list(
            [homework], [assignment]))

        assert result[0].assignment_group is homework
@


\section{Naming a grading scheme in a file}
\label{sec:grading-standard-spec}

An assignment graded with letters, [[grading_type]] [[letter_grade]] or
[[gpa_scale]], turns its points into a grade through a grading scheme, which
the Assignments API calls a grading standard.
A course defines its own schemes, such as P/F/Fx, and an assignment that
names none gets Canvas's default A--F scheme.
A course round rebuilt from files must therefore say which scheme each
assignment uses, or every letter-graded assignment falls back to A--F and
its grades come out wrong (issue~\#557).

The API's field, [[grading_standard_id]], has the problem of
[[assignment_group_id]]: a course's schemes are copied with the course and
get new IDs there, so a file carrying the ID would point the next round at
the old round's scheme.
We therefore solve it as for the group (\cref{sec:assignment-group-spec}).
A file names the scheme by a regex on its title or ID,
\begin{verbatim}
grading_standard: ^P/F/Fx$
\end{verbatim}
an export writes the title, escaped and anchored, and a write resolves the
regex against the course's schemes, with the same errors before anything
is written and the same exception for equally named schemes.
The key is the API's field without its [[_id]], as [[assignment_group]] is,
so the Assignments API documentation still says what it means.

As for the other grading settings, an empty key leaves the scheme alone.
Canvas has a way back to its default scheme, an empty
[[grading_standard_id]] (sandbox, October 2026), but the default has no title
to name, so an export of an assignment that uses it has nothing to write,
and pushing that export must not change the scheme of another assignment.
A file therefore cannot switch an assignment back to the default scheme;
that takes the web interface.

We list the schemes with [[get_grading_standards]], which gives those the
course itself defines.
In fifteen KTH courses that listing held no scheme from the account, so we
cannot tell whether Canvas would list one there.
A scheme the listing does not show behaves like a deleted assignment group:
the export writes no spec, and a push leaves the scheme alone.

Unlike the assignment groups, which every caller lists anyway, the schemes
are a listing of their own, which Canvas may refuse a role or fail to
deliver.
The two directions treat that failure as they treat a scheme that is not
there.
A write turns it into the [[ValueError]] its callers already report before
writing anything, rather than letting a [[CanvasException]] end the command
halfway through a list of files.
An export warns and writes no spec, so that a view or a quiz export still
shows the rest of the item; pushing that file back then leaves the scheme
alone.
<<functions>>=
def course_grading_standards(course):
  """Return the grading standards of `course` as a list.

  Raises ValueError when Canvas does not list them.
  """
  try:
    return list(course.get_grading_standards())
  except canvasapi.exceptions.CanvasException as e:
    raise ValueError(f"grading_standard: cannot list the course's grading "
                     f"standards: {e}")

def resolve_grading_standard(course, spec, current=None):
  """Return the ID of the one grading standard of `course` matching `spec`.

  `spec` is a regex matched against the standards' titles and IDs, or an
  integer ID. `current` is the ID of the standard in use now, kept when
  `spec` matches several standards including it.
  Raises ValueError when no standard or several standards match, when the
  regex is invalid, or when the standards cannot be listed.
  """
  return resolve_named_item(course_grading_standards(course), spec,
                            'grading_standard', 'grading standard', current,
                            name_attr='title')

def grading_standard_spec(course, standard_id):
  """Return an anchored regex naming grading standard `standard_id`.

  Returns None when `standard_id` is None or names no standard of `course`,
  and, after a warning, when the standards cannot be listed.
  """
  if standard_id is None:
    return None
  try:
    standards = course_grading_standards(course)
  except ValueError as e:
    canvaslms.cli.warn(str(e))
    return None
  return named_item_spec(standards, standard_id, name_attr='title')
@

