\subsection{Assignment metadata}

Now let's look at the metadata to add.
<<format assignment metadata>>=
# {assignment.name}

## Metadata

- Unlocks: {canvaslms.cli.utils.format_local_time(assignment.unlock_at) if assignment.unlock_at else None}
- Due:     {canvaslms.cli.utils.format_local_time(assignment.due_at) if assignment.due_at else None}
- Locks:   {canvaslms.cli.utils.format_local_time(assignment.lock_at) if assignment.lock_at else None}
- Ungraded submissions: {assignment.needs_grading_count}
- Submission type: {assignment.submission_types}
- URL: {assignment.html_url}
- Submissions: {assignment.submissions_download_url}
@

\subsection{Assignment rubric}

We want to format the rubric as well.
<<try to add rubric>>=
try:
  text += f"## Rubric\n\n{format_rubric(assignment.rubric)}\n\n"
except AttributeError:
  pass
@

We'll do this with [[format_rubric]].
It should output a markdown representation of the rubric.
Some criteria may lack ratings (for instance, free-form criteria where the
grader enters a custom point value), so we guard against [[None]] ratings.
<<functions>>=
def format_rubric(rubric):
  """
  Returns a markdown representation of the rubric
  """
  if not rubric:
    return "No rubric set."

  text = ""
  for criterion in rubric:
    text += f"- {criterion['description']}\n"
    text += f"  - Points: {criterion['points']}\n"
    text += f"  - Ratings: "
    ratings = criterion.get("ratings") or []
    if ratings:
      text += "; ".join([
        f"{rating['description'].strip()} ({rating['points']})"
        for rating in ratings
      ]) + "\n"
    else:
      text += "(free-form)\n"
    text += f"\n```\n{criterion['long_description']}\n```\n\n"

  return text
@

\subsection{Assignment data as raw JSON}

We also want to format the assignment data as JSON.
We must extract all attributes from the assignment object.
<<functions>>=
def format_json(assignment):
  """Returns a JSON representation of the assignment"""
  return json.dumps({
    key: str(value) for key, value in assignment.__dict__.items()
      if not key.startswith("_")
  }, indent=2)
@


\section{Producing a list of assignments}%
\label{list-assignments-function}

We provide the following functions:
\begin{itemize}
  \item [[list_assignments]], which returns all assignments;
  \item [[list_ungraded_assignments]], which returns all ungraded assignments;
  \item [[filter_assignments]], which returns all assignments whose title
  matches a regular expression.
\end{itemize}
We return the assignments for a list of courses, since we can match several
courses with a regular expression (using [[filter_courses]]).

Here too, the tempting server-side [[bucket="ungraded"]] filter is unsafe.
The submission cache does not track that restrictive query, so an ungraded-only
fetch can make later plain assignment work look empty. We therefore fetch the
ordinary assignment list and, when needed, inspect submissions locally using
the shared [[submissions.is_ungraded()]] predicate.

We still attach [[assignment.course]] and [[assignment.assignment_group]]
before filtering so cached assignment objects expose the same attributes
regardless of which listing path fetched them.
Exactly which of the two we can attach depends on the container at hand, so
we defer that question, and its answer, to the two chunks that follow.
<<functions>>=
def list_assignments(assignments_containers, ungraded=False):
  """Lists all assignments in all assignments containers (courses or
  assignement groups)"""
  for container in assignments_containers:
    <<identify the [[container]]'s course and group>>

    assignments = container.get_assignments()

    for assignment in assignments:
      assignment = canvaslms.cli.utils.ensure_assignment_attrs(assignment)
      <<attach container context to assignment>>

      if ungraded and not has_ungraded_submissions(assignment):
        continue

      yield assignment

def list_ungraded_assignments(assignments_containers):
  return list_assignments(assignments_containers, ungraded=True)
@

\subsection{Attaching container context, one container at a time}

The two containers carry different amounts of context.
A course tells us only which course an assignment belongs to.
An assignment group tells us both the group and, through the [[course]]
attribute that [[filter_assignment_groups]] attaches to it, the course as
well.
So the context to attach is a property of the \emph{current} container, and
the listing must attach whatever this container determines---and nothing
else.

The natural way to say \enquote{nothing else} in Python would seem to be to
leave the name unassigned and catch the resulting error, and that is what an
earlier version of this function did:
\begin{minted}{python}
try:
  assignment.assignment_group = assignment_group
except NameError:
  pass
\end{minted}
This is the wrong tool, because [[NameError]] answers a different question
than the one we are asking.
It reports whether the name is bound anywhere in the function's scope, not
whether \emph{this} iteration bound it---and a local variable outlives the
loop iteration that created it.
So the guard holds only until the first assignment group appears in
[[assignments_containers]].
From that container onward [[assignment_group]] stays bound, the
[[except NameError]] branch becomes unreachable, and every subsequent plain
course has its assignments stamped with the previous [[container]]'s group.

We therefore make the per-iteration question explicit.
Clearing both names at the top of each iteration turns \enquote{not
determined by this container} into an ordinary value we can test, so a
container that determines no group leaves [[assignment_group]] as [[None]]
no matter what earlier containers did.
<<identify the [[container]]'s course and group>>=
course = assignment_group = None

if isinstance(container, canvasapi.course.Course):
  course = container
elif isinstance(container, canvasapi.assignment.AssignmentGroup):
  assignment_group = container
  course = assignment_group.course
@

Attaching is then a pair of ordinary conditionals.
Note that we skip the attribute rather than assigning [[None]] to it: the
assignment objects are cached and shared, so an earlier listing path may
already have attached a correct group, and overwriting it with [[None]]
would lose information that no later path can recover.
<<attach container context to assignment>>=
if course is not None:
  assignment.course = course

if assignment_group is not None:
  assignment.assignment_group = assignment_group
@

Let's verify that context no longer leaks across containers.
The mixed list below is the smallest case that exercises the bug: the
assignment group comes first and binds [[assignment_group]], and the plain
course that follows must still yield assignments with no group attached.

<<test functions>>=
def test_container_context_does_not_leak_across_containers():
    """Each container attaches only the context it determines itself."""
    group_course = Course(MagicMock(), {"id": 11, "name": "Group course"})
    group = AssignmentGroup(MagicMock(), {"id": 5, "name": "Graded"})
    group.course = group_course
    plain_course = Course(MagicMock(), {"id": 12, "name": "Plain course"})

    grouped = Assignment(MagicMock(), {"id": 1, "name": "Lab 1"})
    ungrouped = Assignment(MagicMock(), {"id": 2, "name": "Lab 2"})
    group.get_assignments = Mock(return_value=[grouped])
    plain_course.get_assignments = Mock(return_value=[ungrouped])

    result = list(list_assignments([group, plain_course]))

    assert result == [grouped, ungrouped]
    assert grouped.assignment_group is group
    assert grouped.course is group_course
    assert ungrouped.course is plain_course
    assert not hasattr(ungrouped, "assignment_group")
@

We determine whether an assignment is ungraded by looking at its submissions,
not at summary counters. That keeps the assignment command aligned with the
submission command's resubmission semantics.
<<functions>>=
def has_ungraded_submissions(assignment):
  """Return True when ``assignment`` has at least one ungraded submission."""
  for submission in assignment.get_submissions():
    submission = canvaslms.cli.utils.ensure_submission_attrs(submission)
    if submissions.is_ungraded(submission):
      return True
  return False
@

\subsection{Verifying local ungraded assignment filtering}

The regression fix here has two observable effects: [[list_assignments()]] must
stop passing [[bucket="ungraded"]], and the local filter must still treat
resubmitted work as ungraded.

<<test functions>>=
class TestListAssignments:
    """Test assignment listing helpers."""

    def make_course(self):
        """Create a real Canvas course instance for isinstance checks."""
        return Course(MagicMock(), {"id": 11, "name": "Course"})

    def make_assignment(self, assignment_id, name):
        """Create a real Canvas assignment instance for helper tests."""
        return Assignment(MagicMock(), {"id": assignment_id, "name": name})

    def test_ungraded_listing_uses_plain_assignment_fetch(self):
        """Ungraded assignment listing should not use Canvas bucket filters."""
