We also want to filter out assignments on the title based on regex.
We also take an optional default argument to indicate whether we only want 
ungraded assignments.
<<functions>>=
def filter_assignments(assignments_containers, regex, ungraded=False):
  """Returns all assignments from assignments_container whose
  title matches regex.

  We normalize a missing regex (None or empty string) to '.*' to match all.
  This handles required-filter contexts where -M/-A are used without -a.
  """
  if regex is None or regex == "":
    regex = ".*"
  p = re.compile(regex)
  for assignment in list_assignments(assignments_containers, ungraded=ungraded):
    if p.search(assignment.name or ''):
      yield assignment
    elif p.search(str(assignment.id)):
      yield assignment
@


\section{The [[assignments set-dates]] subcommand}%
\label{set-dates-command}

We want to provide a command to change the various dates on assignments: 
deadlines and availability. The Canvas LMS has three date fields for assignments:
\begin{itemize}
\item [[due_at]] -- when the assignment is due
\item [[unlock_at]] -- when the assignment becomes available to students
\item [[lock_at]] -- when the assignment is no longer available for submission
\end{itemize}

These dates should be provided in a human-readable format and will be converted 
to ISO8601 format for the Canvas API.

The [[assignments_set_dates_command]] function processes the assignments and updates their 
dates according to the provided arguments.
<<functions>>=
def assignments_set_dates_command(config, canvas, args):
  assignment_list = process_assignment_option(canvas, args)
  <<process date arguments>>
  <<update assignment dates>>
@

\subsection{Date options for the assignments set-dates subcommand}

We provide options for each of the three date fields. Users can set one or more 
dates, or clear them by using the special value "none" or "clear".
<<set up date options for set-dates>>=
date_group = set_dates_parser.add_argument_group("date options")
date_group.add_argument("--due-at",
  help="Set the due date (when assignment is due). "
    "Use 'none' or 'clear' to remove the due date.")
date_group.add_argument("--unlock-at", 
  help="Set the unlock date (when assignment becomes available). "
    "Use 'none' or 'clear' to remove the unlock date.")
date_group.add_argument("--lock-at",
  help="Set the lock date (when assignment is no longer available). "
    "Use 'none' or 'clear' to remove the lock date.")
@

\subsection{Processing date arguments}

The date processing logic converts user-provided dates into the structure 
required by the Canvas API. Canvas expects assignment updates to be nested 
under an "assignment" key, and dates must be in UTC format ending with "Z".

We need to parse the date arguments and convert them to the appropriate format 
for the Canvas API. We use the [[arrow]] library for flexible date parsing.

Note: Canvas API expects assignment data to be nested under an 'assignment' key.
<<process date arguments>>=
assignment_data = {}

<<parse due date argument>>
<<parse unlock date argument>>  
<<parse lock date argument>>

if not assignment_data:
  logger.error("No date options provided. Use --due-at, --unlock-at, or --lock-at.")
  return

# Wrap the assignment data in the structure Canvas expects
updates = {"assignment": assignment_data}
@

For each date argument we first check whether the user passed it at all; only
then does the field enter [[assignment_data]]. That guard is what keeps an
omitted option from disturbing a date nobody mentioned: the update we send
contains exactly the fields named on the command line, and Canvas leaves every
other field alone.

Each named field is then converted by [[parse_date]], which accepts the
many human-readable formats the rest of the tool accepts and, in addition,
understands a request to clear the date. That second job turns out to be
subtler than it looks, so it gets its own discussion in
\cref{clearing-a-date}.
<<parse due date argument>>=
if args.due_at is not None:
  try:
    assignment_data['due_at'] = canvaslms.cli.utils.parse_date(args.due_at)
  except ValueError as e:
    logger.error(f"Error parsing due date: {e}")
    return
<<parse unlock date argument>>=
if args.unlock_at is not None:
  try:
    assignment_data['unlock_at'] = canvaslms.cli.utils.parse_date(args.unlock_at)
  except ValueError as e:
    logger.error(f"Error parsing unlock date: {e}")
    return
<<parse lock date argument>>=
if args.lock_at is not None:
  try:
    assignment_data['lock_at'] = canvaslms.cli.utils.parse_date(args.lock_at)
  except ValueError as e:
    logger.error(f"Error parsing lock date: {e}")
    return
@

\subsection{Clearing a date}%
\label{clearing-a-date}

A user clears a date by passing [[none]] or [[clear]] as its value.
[[parse_date]] translates that request into the empty string---the
Canvas wire encoding of \enquote{no date}---rather than [[None]],
precisely so that this command can hand the result to the API
unchanged.
The distinction matters because [[requests]] drops form fields whose
value is [[None]] before the request is sent: a [[None]] date would
make the field vanish from the [[PUT]] entirely, Canvas would receive
nothing to change, and the command would report success while leaving
the date untouched---which is exactly the silent failure this command
used to exhibit.
The empty string instead survives encoding as
[[assignment%5Bdue_at%5D=]], which Canvas reads as an instruction to
clear the date.
The full reasoning lives with [[parse_date]] in the utilities chapter;
here it is enough that whatever [[parse_date]] returns is safe to send.

Let us verify that the translated value really does survive encoding
into a request body.
This is the test that would have caught the original bug: it drives the
exact pipeline, [[combine_kwargs]] followed by the [[requests]]
encoder, that used to swallow the field.
<<test functions>>=
class TestClearedDateEncoding:
    """Test that a cleared date survives into the request body."""

    def test_cleared_date_survives_form_encoding(self):
        """A cleared field must still be present in the request body."""
        updates = {"assignment": {"due_at": parse_date("none")}}
        request = requests.Request(
            "PUT", "https://example.com/api/v1/courses/1/assignments/2",
            data=combine_kwargs(**updates))

        assert "assignment%5Bdue_at%5D=" in request.prepare().body
@


\subsection{Updating assignment dates}

Finally, we iterate through the selected assignments and update their dates
using the Canvas API.  When we report what we are about to do, we recognise a
clearing request by the empty string that \cref{clearing-a-date} introduced;
any other value is a timestamp we are setting.

Each step is reported at [[INFO]] level, so the global [[-v]] flag turns the
running commentary on.
This subcommand once declared a [[-v]]/[[--verbose]] of its own alongside the
date options above, which no code ever read; all it accomplished was to
overwrite the global count with its own [[False]] default and thereby suppress
the very messages it was named after (\cref{sec:global-options-are-global}).
<<update assignment dates>>=
for assignment in assignment_list:
  logger.info(f"Updating assignment '{assignment.name}' (ID: {assignment.id})")
  for field, value in assignment_data.items():
    if value:
      logger.info(f"  Setting {field} to {value}")
    else:
      logger.info(f"  Clearing {field}")

  try:
    assignment.edit(**updates)
    <<update assignment cache after edit>>
    logger.info(f"Updated dates for assignment '{assignment.name}'")
  except Exception as e:
    logger.error(f"Error updating assignment '{assignment.name}' (ID: {assignment.id}): {e}")
@

\subsection{Updating the cache after editing}

After successfully editing an assignment, we must update the cache entry to
prevent unnecessary re-fetches.
The Canvas API's [[edit()]] method updates the object in-place, but the
[[_fetched_at]] timestamp (used for staleness checks) isn't automatically set.
<<update assignment cache after edit>>=
assignment._fetched_at = datetime.now()
if hasattr(assignment.course, 'assignment_cache'):
  assignment.course.assignment_cache[assignment.id] = (assignment, {})
@


\section{The [[assignments edit]] command}

The [[edit]] command updates assignment content. It supports two workflows:

\begin{description}
\item[Interactive editing] When no [[-f]] option is given, the command opens
  each matching assignment in your editor with the current content pre-populated.
  After editing, it shows a preview and asks whether to accept the changes, edit
  further, or discard. This enables quick edits without leaving the terminal.
\item[File-based editing] When [[-f]] is provided, the command reads content
  from a Markdown file with YAML front matter and updates directly. This enables
  a Git-based workflow: export with [[view]], edit locally, push back with
  [[edit -f]].
\end{description}

The command uses the content module's schema-based approach, allowing the same
infrastructure to work across announcements, assignments, and pages.
<<functions>>=
def assignments_edit_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Edit assignment content interactively or from file."""
  if args.file:
    <<edit assignments from file>>
  else:
    <<edit assignments interactively>>

@

\subsection{File-based editing}

When [[-f]] is provided, we read from the file, validate, and update directly.
This mode is script-friendly and does not require user interaction.
<<edit assignments from file>>=
<<read and validate assignment content from file>>
<<update assignment with new content>>
@

We read the file using the content module's infrastructure, then validate the
attributes against the assignment schema. The body content may be Markdown
(default) or HTML (when [[--html]] is specified).

The [[modules]] list is not part of the schema, so we validate it separately
with [[validate_module_specs]] (\cref{sec:module-positions}) and fold its
messages into the same error list, for the reason given for pages: the
module update runs after the description has been written, so a malformed
spec must be caught before anything is sent to Canvas.
<<read and validate assignment content from file>>=
try:
  # body_content is Markdown or HTML depending on args.html
  attributes, body_content = canvaslms.cli.content.read_content_from_file(args.file)
except FileNotFoundError:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, f"File not found: {args.file}")
except Exception as e:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, f"Error reading file: {e}")

errors = canvaslms.cli.content.validate_attributes(
    attributes, canvaslms.cli.content.ASSIGNMENT_SCHEMA)
if 'modules' in attributes:
  errors += modules.validate_module_specs(attributes['modules'])
if errors:
  for error in errors:
    logger.error(f"Validation error: {error}")
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, "File validation failed")
@

\subsection{Updating assignment with new content}

We convert the content to HTML for Canvas (unless [[--html]] is specified, in
which case the content is already HTML).

The matching strategy prioritizes reliability and safety:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If the YAML contains a [[regex]] field, we compile it and match against
  assignment names and Canvas numeric IDs. This provides stable identification
  even when names change, as long as the new name still matches the pattern.
\item If no [[regex]] is in the YAML but a [[name]] is present, we use exact
  name matching. This is safe because it won't accidentally match multiple
  assignments.
\item As a last resort (for backward compatibility), we fall back to
  filter-based matching using [[-a]], [[-A]], and [[-M]] options only if no
  name is specified.
\end{enumerate}
<<update assignment with new content>>=
if args.html:
  html_content = body_content
else:
  html_content = canvaslms.cli.content.markdown_to_html(body_content)

if 'regex' in attributes and attributes['regex']:
  <<update or create assignment by regex>>
else:
  <<update assignments by filter matching>>
@

\subsubsection{Regex-based assignment identification}

When the YAML contains a [[regex]] field, we use [[match_item_by_regex]] from
the content module to find the matching assignment. The function handles the
three-way dispatch (one match, zero matches, multiple matches) and raises
[[EmptyListError]] or [[MultipleMatchesError]] as appropriate.

We match the regex against both assignment names and Canvas numeric IDs,
providing flexibility: an assignment can be identified by a pattern in its name
(\enquote{Lab 1.*}) or by its numeric ID (\verb|^12345$|).

If exactly one assignment matches, we update it. If no assignments match and
[[--create]] is specified, we create a new one. If multiple assignments match,
the exception message lists the matching names so the user can make the regex
more specific.
<<update or create assignment by regex>>=
regex_pattern = attributes['regex']
course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)
if not course_list:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, "No courses found matching criteria")

course = course_list[0]

all_assignments = list(list_assignments([course]))

try:
  assignment = canvaslms.cli.content.match_item_by_regex(
      all_assignments, regex_pattern,
      [lambda a: a.name or '', lambda a: str(a.id)],
      item_type="assignment",
      name_func=lambda a: a.name or '')
  <<update existing assignment>>
except re.error as e:
  canvaslms.cli.err(
      1, f"Invalid regex pattern '{regex_pattern}': {e}")
except canvaslms.cli.EmptyListError:
  if args.create:
    <<create new assignment>>
  else:
    canvaslms.cli.err(1,
      f"No assignments match regex '{regex_pattern}'. "
      f"Use --create to create a new assignment.")
@

When updating an existing assignment, we build the update dictionary and call
the Canvas API.
The symbolic links are resolved here, against the assignment's course,
because the body was converted once for the file but is written to the
course of each matched assignment (\cref{sec:content-links}).
<<update existing assignment>>=
update_data = {
  'assignment': {
    'name': attributes.get('name', assignment.name),
    'description': canvaslms.cli.content.resolve_body_for(
        html_content, assignment.course, args),
  }
}

<<add optional assignment attributes to update>>

try:
  assignment.edit(**update_data)
  <<update assignment cache after edit>>
  logger.info(f"Updated assignment: {assignment.name}")
  <<update assignment module membership>>
except Exception as e:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1,
    f"Error updating assignment '{assignment.name}': {e}")
@

\subsubsection{Creating new assignments}

When [[--create]] is specified and the assignment doesn't exist, we create a
new one. We use the Canvas API's [[create_assignment]] method.
The optional attributes come from [[assignment_attributes_to_params]]
(\cref{sec:assignment-attributes-to-params}), told that it is creating, so
that an empty date is left out rather than sent as a removal.
<<create new assignment>>=
create_params = {
  'name': attributes.get('name', 'Untitled Assignment'),
  'description': canvaslms.cli.content.resolve_body_for(
      html_content, course, args),
}
try:
  create_params.update(
      assignment_attributes_to_params(attributes, course, creating=True))
except ValueError as e:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, f"Cannot create assignment: {e}")

try:
  new_assignment = course.create_assignment(assignment=create_params)
  new_assignment.course = course
  logger.info(f"Created assignment: {new_assignment.name} "
    f"(id: {new_assignment.id})")
  <<update new assignment module membership>>
except Exception as e:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, f"Error creating assignment: {e}")
@

For newly created assignments, we also process module membership if specified.
<<update new assignment module membership>>=
if 'modules' in attributes:
  module_specs = attributes['modules']
  added, removed, moved, required = modules.update_item_modules(
      new_assignment.course, 'Assignment', new_assignment.id, module_specs)
  modules.print_module_changes(added, removed, moved, required)
@

\subsubsection{Filter-based assignment matching}

When no [[regex]] is specified in the YAML, we check whether a [[name]] is
present. If so, we use exact name matching to find the assignment---this is
critical for safety. Without it, the command would fall back to filter-based
matching with the default [[-a '.*']] pattern, which matches \emph{all}
assignments in the course. The result would be catastrophic: every assignment
would be overwritten with the content from the file.

If no [[name]] is present either, we fall back to filter-based matching using
the [[-a]], [[-A]], and [[-M]] options (the original behavior). To prevent
accidental mass-updates, we refuse to proceed if the filter matches more than
one assignment.
<<update assignments by filter matching>>=
if 'name' in attributes and attributes['name']:
  <<update or create assignment by name>>
else:
  assignment_list = process_assignment_option(canvas, args)
  if len(assignment_list) > 1:
    canvaslms.cli.err(1,
      f"{len(assignment_list)} assignments match filter. "
      f"Add 'name' or 'regex' to YAML, or use -a to narrow selection.")
  for assignment in assignment_list:
    <<update existing assignment>>
@

\subsubsection{Name-based assignment identification}

When no [[regex]] is specified in the YAML but a [[name]] is present, we use
[[match_item_by_name]] from the content module for exact name matching. This
avoids the catastrophic scenario where the default [[-a '.*']] pattern matches
all assignments and overwrites them all with the file content.

If exactly one assignment matches, we update it. If none match and [[--create]]
is specified, we create a new assignment. If multiple assignments share the same
name, the exception tells the user to add a [[regex]] field to disambiguate.
<<update or create assignment by name>>=
name = attributes['name']
course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)
if not course_list:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, "No courses found matching criteria")

course = course_list[0]

all_assignments = list(list_assignments([course]))

try:
  assignment = canvaslms.cli.content.match_item_by_name(
      all_assignments, name,
      lambda a: a.name or '',
      item_type="assignment")
  <<update existing assignment>>
except canvaslms.cli.EmptyListError:
  if args.create:
    <<create new assignment>>
  else:
    canvaslms.cli.err(1,
      f"No assignments match name '{name}'. "
      f"Use --create to create a new assignment.")
@

\subsubsection{From front matter to assignment parameters}
\label{sec:assignment-attributes-to-params}

Three paths write front matter to Canvas: updating an existing assignment
from a file, creating one with [[--create]], and accepting an interactive
edit.
They used to carry three copies of the same block of [[if]]s, and a defect
in that block was therefore three defects.
They now share one function,
[[assignment_attributes_to_params]], which turns the optional attributes into
the fields of the [[assignment]] parameter; each path adds only the
[[name]] and [[description]], which it derives in its own way.

The function distinguishes three states of an attribute in the front matter,
because the user means three different things by them.
\begin{description}
\item[Absent] The file says nothing about the attribute, so we send nothing
  and Canvas keeps its value.
  This lets a file update the description alone.
\item[Present with a value] We send the value, converted for Canvas.
\item[Present but empty] For a date, [[due_at: null]] (which is also how
  [[assignments view]] exports an assignment without that date) or
  [[due_at: '']] asks for the date to be removed.
\end{description}
The third state is where the old block failed (issue~\#527): it tested
[[is not None]] and so treated an emptied date like an absent one.
A snapshot restored from a file therefore kept whatever date the assignment
had acquired since, the command still reported success, and a due date moved
before a stale [[unlock_at]] could not be fixed from the file at all, since
Canvas refuses a due date outside the availability window.

We send a removal as the empty string, the wire encoding of \enquote{no
date} that \cref{clearing-a-date} explains: a [[None]] would be dropped by
[[requests]] before it reached Canvas.
Reading an empty date as a removal is safe for a file that is current,
because [[assignments view]] and the interactive editor fill in every date
the assignment has: a [[null]] that nobody touched stands for a date that is
already absent, and removing it again changes nothing.
It is not safe for a stale file.
A file exported before someone set a date in the web interface still says
[[due_at: null]], and pushing it now removes that date, where it used to be
kept by accident.
We accept that, because it is what the file says and what it already did to
every date it does set: the file is the authority over the attributes it
names, and a file kept in a course repository must be refreshed from Canvas
after edits made in the web interface, or those edits are undone.

Creating is the exception.
A new assignment has no date to remove, so the [[creating]] flag makes the
function leave empty dates out, which keeps the creation request to what the
file actually sets.

[[points_possible]] and [[published]] keep their old reading.
An empty [[points_possible]] is left out, for the reason that empty quiz
settings are (\cref{sec:quiz-null-attributes}): we know of no Canvas
spelling that would clear it, and a [[null]] sent as [[None]] would vanish
anyway.
[[published]] has no empty state worth the name, so it is sent whenever it
is present.
An [[assignment_group]] spec (\cref{sec:assignment-group-spec}) is resolved
against the course to the [[assignment_group_id]] Canvas expects, which is
why the function takes the course, and the assignment's present group for
the ambiguous case; an empty spec, [[null]] or [['']], leaves the group
alone, since an assignment cannot be in no group.
A [[grading_standard]] spec (\cref{sec:grading-standard-spec}) is resolved
to a [[grading_standard_id]] the same way, with the assignment's present
scheme for the ambiguous case.
A spec that names no group, or several, raises [[ValueError]], as an
unparseable date does, and each caller turns that into a message before it
writes anything.

The grading settings, [[grading_type]], [[omit_from_final_grade]] and the
others in [[ASSIGNMENT_API_SETTINGS]] (issue~\#555), need none of this.
They are fields of the Assignments API under their own names, so each is
sent as the file gives it, and the Canvas documentation says what the
values mean.
A [[null]] is left out, as for [[points_possible]]: the template of a new
assignment lists every key with an empty value, and only the settings the
file fills in should reach Canvas.
Canvas itself sets one limit.
When an assignment is created without an online submission type, which is
the case for one created from a file, Canvas drops [[allowed_attempts]] and
keeps its default of unlimited ($-1$); an edit of the same assignment keeps
the value (sandbox, October 2026).
Attempts only count for online submissions, so nothing is lost, but a file
pushed with [[--create]] and then exported shows $-1$ where it said
otherwise.

Date attributes require special handling: what YAML's [[safe_load]] yields
depends on how complete the timestamp is.
Only values matching YAML's timestamp resolver become Python objects:
\verb|due_at: 2026-03-06 19:00:00| (with seconds) yields a
[[datetime.datetime]] and \verb|due_at: 2026-03-06| a [[datetime.date]],
whereas \verb|due_at: 2026-03-06 19:00| (no seconds) stays a plain string.
The Canvas API, on the other hand, expects ISO~8601 UTC strings
ending with~``Z''.
We use [[format_canvas_time]] to perform this conversion; it
handles all three input types, so users need not know which form YAML
happened to parse.
<<constants>>=
ASSIGNMENT_DATE_ATTRIBUTES = ('due_at', 'unlock_at', 'lock_at')
<<functions>>=
def assignment_attributes_to_params(attributes, course=None,
                                    creating=False, current_group=None,
                                    current_standard=None):
  """Return the optional `assignment` API fields that `attributes` set.

  An absent attribute is left out, so Canvas keeps its value. A date
  that is present but null or empty is sent as '', which clears it in
  Canvas; with `creating` it is left out instead, since a new
  assignment has no date to clear. An `assignment_group` spec is
  resolved against `course` to an `assignment_group_id`; `current_group`
  is the assignment's present group, kept when the spec is ambiguous.
  A `grading_standard` spec is resolved to a `grading_standard_id` in the
  same way, with `current_standard`.
  Raises ValueError for a date or spec Canvas cannot be given.
  """
  params = {}
  for key in ASSIGNMENT_DATE_ATTRIBUTES:
    if key not in attributes:
      continue
    value = attributes[key]
    if value is None or value == '':
      if not creating:
        params[key] = ''
    else:
      params[key] = canvaslms.cli.utils.format_canvas_time(value)
  if attributes.get('points_possible') is not None:
    params['points_possible'] = attributes['points_possible']
  if 'published' in attributes:
    params['published'] = attributes['published']
  if attributes.get('assignment_group') not in (None, ''):
    params['assignment_group_id'] = resolve_assignment_group(
        course, attributes['assignment_group'], current_group)
  if attributes.get('grading_standard') not in (None, ''):
    params['grading_standard_id'] = resolve_grading_standard(
        course, attributes['grading_standard'], current_standard)
  for key in canvaslms.cli.content.ASSIGNMENT_API_SETTINGS:
    if attributes.get(key) is not None:
      params[key] = attributes[key]
  return params
@

The update path merges these fields into the parameters it has already
built from the name and the description.
<<add optional assignment attributes to update>>=
try:
  update_data['assignment'].update(assignment_attributes_to_params(
      attributes, assignment.course,
      current_group=getattr(assignment, 'assignment_group_id', None),
      current_standard=getattr(assignment, 'grading_standard_id', None)))
except ValueError as e:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, f"Cannot update assignment '{assignment.name}': {e}")
@

Let's verify the three states of a date, and that creating leaves empty
dates out:
<<test functions>>=
def test_absent_date_is_left_out():
  assert assignment_attributes_to_params({'name': 'Lab'}) == {}

def test_null_and_empty_dates_clear():
  params = assignment_attributes_to_params(
      {'due_at': None, 'unlock_at': ''})
  assert params == {'due_at': '', 'unlock_at': ''}

def test_present_date_is_converted_for_canvas():
  import datetime as dt
  params = assignment_attributes_to_params(
      {'due_at': dt.datetime(2026, 12, 1, 10, 0, tzinfo=dt.timezone.utc)})
  assert params == {'due_at': '2026-12-01T10:00:00Z'}

def test_creating_leaves_empty_dates_out():
  params = assignment_attributes_to_params(
      {'due_at': None, 'lock_at': '', 'published': False}, creating=True)
  assert params == {'published': False}

def test_empty_points_possible_is_left_out():
  assert assignment_attributes_to_params({'points_possible': None}) == {}

def test_empty_grading_standard_leaves_the_scheme_alone():
  course = course_with_standards((101, "P/F"))
  assert assignment_attributes_to_params(
      {'grading_standard': ''}, course) == {}
  assert assignment_attributes_to_params(
      {'grading_standard': None}, course) == {}

def test_grading_settings_are_sent_as_the_api_names_them():
  params = assignment_attributes_to_params(
      {'grading_type': 'pass_fail', 'omit_from_final_grade': True,
       'allowed_attempts': -1, 'peer_reviews': False,
       'automatic_peer_reviews': None})
  assert params == {'grading_type': 'pass_fail',
                    'omit_from_final_grade': True,
                    'allowed_attempts': -1, 'peer_reviews': False}
@

The test that matters to a user drives the whole file path: a file is read,
matched to an assignment and written, and we look at the form fields that
reach the requester---the last point we own before the request leaves.
The requester is a [[MagicMock]] because a live write cannot run offline;
listing the course's assignments is patched to hand over one real
[[Assignment]], which spares us faking the paginated listing.
<<test functions>>=
def edit_assignment_from_file(tmp_path, front_matter, **assignment_fields):
  """Run `assignments edit -f` on a file; return the form fields sent.

  The assignment in Canvas also has `assignment_fields`.
  """
  requester = MagicMock()
  requester.request.return_value.json.return_value = {}
  course = Course(requester, {"id": 1, "course_code": "CODE"})
  course.get_assignment_groups = lambda: [
      AssignmentGroup(requester, {"id": 31, "name": "Labs"}),
      AssignmentGroup(requester, {"id": 32, "name": "Surveys (INL1)"})]
  course.get_grading_standards = lambda: [
      GradingStandard(requester, {"id": 101, "title": "P/F"}),
      GradingStandard(requester, {"id": 102, "title": "P/F/Fx"})]
  assignment = Assignment(requester, {
      "id": 7, "course_id": 1, "name": "Lab", "description": "",
      "due_at": "2026-11-20T22:59:00Z",
      "unlock_at": "2026-11-09T07:00:00Z",
      "lock_at": "2026-11-27T22:59:00Z", **assignment_fields})
  assignment.course = course
  path = tmp_path / "lab.md"
  path.write_text(f"---\n{front_matter}---\n\nBody\n")

  parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
  add_command(parser.add_subparsers())
  args = parser.parse_args(
      ["assignments", "edit", "-c", "CODE", "-f", str(path)])
  with patch("canvaslms.cli.assignments.courses.process_course_option",
             return_value=[course]), \
       patch("canvaslms.cli.assignments.list_assignments",
             return_value=[assignment]):
    assignments_edit_command({}, MagicMock(), args)

  if not requester.request.called:
    return None
  return dict(requester.request.call_args.kwargs["_kwargs"])

def test_file_with_null_and_empty_dates_clears_them(tmp_path):
  sent = edit_assignment_from_file(
      tmp_path, "regex: ^Lab$\nname: Lab\nunlock_at: null\nlock_at: ''\n")

  assert sent["assignment[unlock_at]"] == ""
  assert sent["assignment[lock_at]"] == ""
  assert "assignment[due_at]" not in sent

def test_file_date_is_sent_in_canvas_format(tmp_path):
  sent = edit_assignment_from_file(
      tmp_path, "regex: ^Lab$\nname: Lab\ndue_at: 2026-12-01T10:00:00Z\n")

  assert sent["assignment[due_at]"] == "2026-12-01T10:00:00Z"
  assert "assignment[unlock_at]" not in sent

def test_file_moves_assignment_to_the_named_group(tmp_path):
  sent = edit_assignment_from_file(
      tmp_path, "regex: ^Lab$\nname: Lab\n"
                "assignment_group: ^Surveys \\(INL1\\)$\n")

  assert sent["assignment[assignment_group_id]"] == 32

def test_file_naming_no_group_writes_nothing(tmp_path):
  with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
    edit_assignment_from_file(
        tmp_path, "regex: ^Lab$\nname: Lab\nassignment_group: ^Exams$\n")

def test_file_grading_settings_reach_canvas(tmp_path):
  sent = edit_assignment_from_file(
      tmp_path, "regex: ^Lab$\nname: Lab\ngrading_type: pass_fail\n"
                "omit_from_final_grade: true\npeer_reviews: null\n")

  assert sent["assignment[grading_type]"] == "pass_fail"
  assert sent["assignment[omit_from_final_grade]"] is True
  assert "assignment[peer_reviews]" not in sent

def test_file_sets_the_named_grading_standard(tmp_path):
  sent = edit_assignment_from_file(
      tmp_path, "regex: ^Lab$\nname: Lab\ngrading_type: letter_grade\n"
                "grading_standard: ^P/F/Fx$\n")

  assert sent["assignment[grading_standard_id]"] == 102
  assert "assignment[grading_standard]" not in sent

def test_file_naming_no_grading_standard_writes_nothing(tmp_path):
  with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
    edit_assignment_from_file(
        tmp_path, "regex: ^Lab$\nname: Lab\ngrading_standard: ^A-E$\n")

def test_file_matching_the_present_scheme_among_others_keeps_it(tmp_path):
  sent = edit_assignment_from_file(
      tmp_path, "regex: ^Lab$\nname: Lab\ngrading_standard: ^P/F\n",
      grading_standard_id=101)

  assert sent["assignment[grading_standard_id]"] == 101
